---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const pageTitle = "Likutay Halachos - Birchas HaRaiach 2";
const pageDescription = "Breslov teachings from Likutay Halachos: Birchas HaRaiach 2";
---
<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="page-banner">
    <h1>Likutay Halachos</h1>
    <p>Orach Chaim 2 - Birchas Haraiach</p>
  </div>
<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=EB+Garamond:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400;1,500&family=Cinzel:wght@400;600;700&family=Noto+Serif+Hebrew&display=swap');
  .lh-container { --ink:#1c1c2e;--parchment:#f7f3ea;--parchment2:#eee8d5;--gold:#a07830;--gold-light:#c8a050;--sapphire:#1e3a5f;--sapphire2:#2e5580;--sapphire-pale:#dde8f5;--ruby:#7a1a2e;--border:#b89860;--muted:#5a5040;--verse-color:#1e3a5f;--source-color:#888070; font-family:'EB Garamond',Georgia,serif; font-size:18px; line-height:1.85; color:var(--ink); background:var(--parchment); text-align:justify; max-width:800px; margin:0 auto; padding:40px 20px 80px; }
  .lh-container .book-title { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 50px; padding-bottom: 30px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border); }
  .lh-container .book-title h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.2em; font-weight: 700; color: var(--sapphire); letter-spacing: 0.03em; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .lh-container .section-header { text-align: center; margin: 50px 0 30px; padding: 15px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .lh-container .halacha-header { text-align: center; margin: 55px 0 20px; padding: 20px 0; border-top: 2px solid var(--gold); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--gold); }
  .lh-container .sa-excerpt { text-align: center; margin: 20px 0 35px; padding: 15px 25px; background: var(--parchment2); border-left: 3px solid var(--gold); border-right: 3px solid var(--gold); font-style: italic; }
  .lh-container .ois { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.15em; color: var(--gold); margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 10px; display: block; }
  .lh-container p { margin-bottom: 18px; text-indent: 1.5em; }
  .lh-container p:first-of-type, .lh-container .ois + p, .lh-container .sa-excerpt + p { text-indent: 0; }
  .lh-container .heb { font-style: italic; color: var(--ruby); }
  .page-banner { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a365d 0%, #2c5282 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem 2rem; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .page-banner h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: white; }
</style>
  <div class="lh-container">

<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaRaiach v'HaHodaah — Halachah 2<br>All 5 Oaseyos<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM II:1</span> (<i>Tik'u BaChodesh Shofar</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — <i>Tefillah</i> and <i>D'var Hashem</i>: Why Smell Ascends Directly to the Source</div>

<div class="p">Regarding smell, one recites a <span class="h">berachah</span> beforehand and does not recite any <span class="h">berachah</span> afterward at all.</div>

<div class="p">This follows the discourse <span class="v">"Tik'u BaChodesh Shofar"</span> (<span class="lr">LM II:1</span>). It is explained there that when one holds to <span class="h">tefillah</span>, one does not need medicines at all, for <span class="h">tefillah</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, which is the root of all the forces of all herbs and plants. There, at the root, everything is one, for the differentiation of forces — one herb healing this and another herb healing that — is only in the branches, when they have already descended to this world and become differentiated. But at their root in <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, which is the force of the earth through which all herbs grow — <span class="v">"He sends His word and melts them; He blows His wind and the waters flow"</span> — there everything is one: all healings, all forces, all variety of tastes and smells.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore when one holds to <span class="h">tefillah</span> (<span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>), one does not need specific medicines, since the <span class="h">tefillah</span> itself is the root of all healings. There everything is one: no difference between one illness and another. All is rectified through the root = <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> = <span class="h">tefillah</span>. The root of all the variety of forces in herbs and plants is one — <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>. The essential force of the earth and all that grows upon it is through <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, as is said: <span class="v">"forever, Hashem, Your word stands in the heavens"</span> — this is the word <span class="v">"let the earth bring forth"</span>, as our Sages explain. This <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> stands and endures forever, and through it all the forces of plants and their healings emerge and are differentiated. See there well.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — The <i>Berachah</i> = <i>D'var Hashem</i>: Elevating What One Eats to Its Source</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">berachah</span> recited on everything is the aspect of <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>. Through it, the thing one eats and drinks is elevated and raised up to its source, as is known. This is the essential purpose of the <span class="h">berachah</span>: to raise and elevate the food one eats to its supernal source. For the <span class="h">berachah</span>, which is <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, is itself the source as above. This is <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>: one blesses and praises Hashem who nourishes and sustains and maintains everything — <span class="v">"He gives food to all flesh"</span> — for the root and life-force of all food is <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> through which all grows. Therefore through the <span class="h">berachah</span>, which is <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, everything ascends to its source.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — First <i>Berachah</i> and After-<i>Berachah</i>: Two Stages of Elevation</div>

<div class="p">This is the matter of first <span class="h">berachah</span> and after-<span class="h">berachah</span>. Through the first <span class="h">berachah</span>, the thing one eats is elevated to its supernal source, to <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> as above. This is the purpose of all first <span class="h">berachos</span> recited on everything: the <span class="h">berachah</span>'s essence is that one mentions in the <span class="h">berachah</span> that Hashem <span class="h">Yisbarach</span> is the One who created this thing and gives it its flavor and sustenance — for He is its root and life-force. By mentioning this, one elevates this thing to its source. Through a first <span class="h">berachah</span> alone, the food is elevated to <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> — but it is still only at the level of a specific force within <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>: the specific force of this particular food. To elevate further, to where everything is one at the root of <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> — there all forces are one, all variety is one, for there is only Hashem alone, the simple Unity — for this, one needs the after-<span class="h">berachah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore in the after-<span class="h">berachah</span> we say: <span class="v">"for the Land and for the food"</span> — for <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is the source of all lands, the essential <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> itself, where there is no more distinction between one force and another but rather the Unity of the Holy One, <span class="h">Baruch Hu</span>. We recite <span class="v">"for the Land and for the food"</span> to elevate everything to the root of <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, where all variety of forces is one. We also mention <span class="v">"for the Land and for the fruits"</span>: through mentioning <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and its fruits, we elevate all foods to their ultimate source.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — Why No After-<i>Berachah</i> on Smell: It Is Already at the Source</div>

<div class="p">Therefore for smell, one needs no after-<span class="h">berachah</span> — only a first <span class="h">berachah</span> alone. For smell is <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> — <span class="mx">"a thing from which the neshamah derives benefit but not the body."</span> The forces of smell are not drawn out and distributed among the body's limbs, for the body has no benefit from it. It is only a benefit of the <span class="h">neshamah</span>. The <span class="h">neshamah</span> does not need distinction or differentiation of forces, for there everything is one. Smell is close to the aspect of <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, which is its root: where everything is one and there is no differentiation whatsoever — as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it needs only a first <span class="h">berachah</span>: through the first <span class="h">berachah</span> it immediately ascends to the root of all forces in <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, where it was already standing almost, since it is a benefit of the <span class="h">neshamah</span> alone and not the body. There is no need for an after-<span class="h">berachah</span> to continue elevating, for through the first <span class="h">berachah</span> alone it has already arrived at its source. But food and drink — whose benefit enters the body and is distributed among the limbs, and is still at the level of this specific force, distinct from other forces — need the after-<span class="h">berachah</span> to elevate to the ultimate source: the root of <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> where all is one.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — <i>Havdalah</i> Spices: The Emergence of <i>Mashiach</i> and Judging by Smell</div>

<div class="p">Based on the above, it is well understood why we recite a <span class="h">berachah</span> on spices at <span class="h">Havdalah</span> on <span class="h">Motzaai Shabbos</span>. This points to the emergence of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, who begins to emerge on <span class="h">Motzaai Shabbos</span>, as is known. As we say: <span class="v">"Eliyahu HaNavi... speedily, may he come to us with Mashiach ben Dovid."</span> On <span class="h">Shabbos</span> there is no need for medicine, for then one holds entirely to <span class="h">tefillah</span>. During the week, when one cannot always be at the level of <span class="h">tefillah</span>, one needs the differentiation of medicine's forces, each according to what he needs. But <span class="h">Mashiach</span> will rectify everything through <span class="h">tefillah</span> — the root, <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> — and there will be no need for medicine's forces at all. As Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> explains: <span class="h">Mashiach</span> will heal the world through smell, as it says: <span class="v">"he shall be imbued with the spirit of the fear of Hashem; he shall not judge by the sight of his eyes nor decide by the hearing of his ears"</span> (Yeshayahu 11). The Gemara expounds: <span class="mx">he judges by smell</span> — <span class="h">morich v'dayain</span>. For smell is the root of all forces, the aspect of <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span> where everything is one, as above. Through this, all will be healed and rectified. <span class="h">Mashiach</span> will merit illuminating that one should hold to <span class="h">tefillah</span> always (the root of all forces), and then no medicine will be needed. Therefore we recite a <span class="h">berachah</span> on spices on <span class="h">Motzaai Shabbos</span> — the emergence of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>: smell is the root of all forces, the aspect of <span class="h">d'var Hashem</span>, through which <span class="h">Mashiach</span> will rectify everything.</div>

<div class="cn">✦ End of Birchas HaRaiach v'HaHodaah, Halachah 2 — All 5 Oaseyos Complete ✦<br><i>Na Nach Nachma Nachman May'Uman</i></div>

  </div>
</Layout>